## Part 6: Your final model

Combine the features and transformations that earned their place, **judged by CV RMSE, not p-values.**

```{r}
#| label: final
# Keep whichever combination earned its place by CV RMSE, not by p-values
candidates <- tibble(
  model   = c("m5", "m6", "m7", "m8", "m9"),
  formula = list(f5, f_log, f_sq, f_cat, f_all),
  cv      = c(cv_m5, cv_m6, cv_m7, cv_m8, cv_m9)
)
candidates %>% select(model, cv)

final_pick <- candidates$model[which.min(candidates$cv)]
f_final <- candidates$formula[[which.min(candidates$cv)]]
f_final

m_final <- lm(f_final, data = tracts)
summary(m_final)

cv_final <- cv_rmse(f_final, tracts)

model_log <- model_log %>%
  add_row(model = "final", added = paste("lowest CV RMSE of m5–m9:", final_pick),
          cv_rmse = cv_final,
          notes = "ACS baseline + rail access + shootings + transformations")

model_log
```

### Where is the final model wrong?

```{r}
#| label: resid-map-2
tracts <- tracts %>% mutate(resid_final = residuals(m_final))

ggplot(tracts) +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = resid_final), color = NA) +
  scale_fill_gradient2(low = "#2166ac", mid = "grey95", high = "#b2182b", midpoint = 0) +
  theme_void() +
  labs(title = "Final model: where it's wrong",
       subtitle = "Red = actual higher than predicted",
       fill = "Residual")
```

Put your two residual maps side by side, or put them next to each other in the rendered document. Did the clusters from Part 2C shrink? Where did they stay?

```{r}
#| label: resid-compare
#| fig-width: 10
#| fig-height: 6
tracts %>%
  select(resid_m1, resid_final) %>%
  pivot_longer(c(resid_m1, resid_final), names_to = "model", values_to = "residual") %>%
  mutate(model = factor(recode(model,
                               resid_m1    = "Baseline (m1)",
                               resid_final = "Final model"),
                        levels = c("Baseline (m1)", "Final model"))) %>%
  ggplot() +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = residual), color = NA) +
  scale_fill_gradient2(low = "#2166ac", mid = "grey95", high = "#b2182b", midpoint = 0) +
  facet_wrap(~ model) +
  theme_void() +
  labs(title = "Where the model is wrong, before and after",
       subtitle = "Red = more car-free households than predicted",
       fill = "Residual (pp)")

c(sd_m1 = sd(tracts$resid_m1), sd_final = sd(tracts$resid_final))
```

*Your answer:* The spread of the residuals falls from a standard deviation of `r round(sd(tracts$resid_m1), 1)` percentage points in the baseline to `r round(sd(tracts$resid_final), 1)` in the final model. [After rendering, describe which red clusters from Part 2C faded (for example, tracts along the Broad Street and Market-Frankford lines) and which remain.]

### Are the biggest misses in the least reliable tracts?

```{r}
#| label: fog
ggplot(tracts, aes(x = y_cv, y = abs(resid_final))) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_smooth(se = FALSE) +
  labs(x = "CV of your outcome estimate (%)", y = "Size of the miss") +
  theme_minimal()
```

