## Part 7: Reflection (about 300 words)

Answer in prose, drawing on your model log (incorporate our words of the day for bonus points. just kidding):

1. **What helped, and what didn't?** Name the feature that lowered CV RMSE the most and the one that disappointed you most. Offer a reason for each.
2. **Read one coefficient twice.** Choose a variable in your final model. Give its β̂ reading and its ŷ reading in one sentence each.
3. **What's still missing?** Look at your final residual map. Name one thing about those places you'd want to measure next, and where you might get the data.
4. **How much of the remaining error might be uncertainty or statistical noise?** Use your last plot.

```{r}
#| label: reflection-numbers
#| include: false
cv_gain       <- cv_m1 - cv_final
noise_share   <- mean(abs(tracts$resid_final) < tracts$y_moe, na.rm = TRUE) * 100
cv_resid_cor  <- cor(tracts$y_cv, abs(tracts$resid_final), use = "complete.obs")
renter_coef   <- coef(m_final)["pct_renter"]
```

*Your answer:*

My baseline model, built only from income, tenure, housing type, and household size, missed car-free shares by about `r round(cv_m1, 1)` percentage points on tracts it had not seen. The final model (`r final_pick`) lowers that to `r round(cv_final, 1)`, a gain of `r round(cv_gain, 1)` points. The spatial feature that helped most was `r best_feature`, likely because `r feature_reason[best_feature]`. The most disappointing was `r worst_feature`, likely because `r feature_reason[worst_feature]`. The model log taught me that a variable can look important on a map and still add nothing once its neighbors are in the model.

Take `pct_renter`. The β̂ reading is that, holding the other variables constant, a tract with 10 more percentage points of renter households has a car-free share about `r abs(round(renter_coef * 10, 1))` points `r if (renter_coef > 0) "higher" else "lower"`, but this is an association, not evidence that renting causes people to give up cars. The ŷ reading is that renter share stays in the model because it carries information about car ownership that lowers out-of-sample error, whether or not the relationship is causal.

The final residual map still shows clusters [name them after rendering]. Next I would measure bus service: stop frequency from SEPTA's 2023 GTFS schedules, since my rail layers ignore the bus network most car-free households rely on. I would also add college enrollment (ACS table B14007), because student neighborhoods are car-free for reasons income cannot explain.

Some of the remaining error is fog, not model failure. For `r round(noise_share)`% of tracts, the final model's miss is smaller than the outcome's own 90% margin of error, so the model cannot be judged wrong there. The correlation between the outcome's CV and the size of the miss is `r round(cv_resid_cor, 2)`, so `r if (cv_resid_cor > 0.2) "the largest misses tend to occur where the estimate is least reliable" else "large misses are not concentrated in the noisiest tracts, so most of the remaining error is a modeling problem"`.

